WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:27.869
THE STATE OF T-MOBILE:
Virtually 

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:34.110
everybody in the entire world recognizes that
ringtone. For many of us, that ringtone is

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:40.390
not just reminiscent of a certain company
but an entire time period, the early 2000s.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:46.290
A time before smartphones and smartwatches
and smart everything. Computers and cell phones

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.460
were just starting to gain popularity amongst
the general public, and ringtones were a fashion

00:00:51.460 --> 00:01:01.469
statement, not something that you would apologize
for. But, as smartphones became more mainstream,

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:07.869
the popularity of ringtones fell off a cliff
and with it went the popularity of T Mobile.

00:01:07.869 --> 00:01:12.939
This trend was only made worse by the 2008
financial recession which caused the stock

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:19.720
to sell off 86%. Things got so bad that T
Mobile even agreed to being bought out by

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:26.850
AT&amp;T for $39 billion in 2011. But, that deal
was stopped by the US government. With nowhere

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:32.650
left to go, T Mobile just continued its death
dive, and during the worst of it, T Mobile

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:39.640
was writing off everything they had leading
to losses of over $7.5 billion. At this point,

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.550
you would think that it was just a matter
of time until T Mobile filed for bankruptcy,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:51.320
but then came a new CEO, John Legere. Hopes
weren’t that high given that John didn’t

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.899
have much to work with, but somehow, over
the next 8 years, John would stage one of

00:01:56.899 --> 00:02:03.500
the biggest comebacks of corporate history.
Since then, T Mobile has grown a mind boggling

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:11.660
1900% or 20X. And they have transitioned from
being the ones who needed a bailout to being

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:17.970
ones handing out bailouts. Likely the best
example of this is their merger, or basically

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:23.300
acquisition, of Sprint which propelled them
to not only be telecom juggernaut, but be

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:30.890
the largest telecom company in the world period.
They currently stand at a valuation of $186

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.780
billion, and fun fact, the recession hasn’t
affected their stock price whatsoever. They’re

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.190
literally floating around all times highs
as we speak. So, here’s how T Mobile went

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:48.370
from being so popular that they were a symbol
of a time period to near bankruptcy to becoming

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.570
the largest telecom company in the world.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:57.840
NOT SO HUMBLE ORIGINS:
Taking a look back, the story of T Mobile

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.990
doesn’t actually start in America, it actually
starts in Germany, and to this day the original

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:09.670
T Mobile or Deutsche Telecom is still a German
giant. In fact, they’re the 6th largest

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:15.599
telecom company in the world and they trace
their roots all the way back to 1866 to Germany’s

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:21.310
national postal service Reichspost. While
the organization was technically a mail service,

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:26.530
they were really more of a communications
business. So, after the invention of telephones,

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:31.220
Reichspost was one of the first to adopt the
technology and spread it across the country.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:36.879
But the real rise of Reichspost didn’t come
till the rise of Mr. Small Moustache in the

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:42.299
1930s and 40s. Mr. Small Moustache was well
aware of just how critical robust communication

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:48.000
was in trying to challenge the world powers.
So he spent a lot of resources to build up

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.019
Reichspost, and before Germany started losing,
Reichspost was stronger than ever. For obvious

00:03:54.019 --> 00:04:00.000
reasons, the allied forces weren’t fans
of this, and after they took over, they would

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.560
shut down Reichspost in 1947. They would split
up the organization into a few smaller organizations,

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.659
the most notable of which were Deutsche Bundespost
in West Germany and Deutsche Post in East

00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:17.190
Germany. These two organizations would essentially
remain as state owned monopolies up until

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.609
the fall of the Berlin Wall. Just a couple
of months before the collapse, West Germany

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:26.729
would split Duetsche Bundespost into three
sectors which included a postal service, a

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:31.970
postal bank, and of course, a communications
service. After the fall of the Berlin Wall,

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:36.990
these sectors would basically absorb the remains
of the East German services. And given that

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:41.810
capitalism was now the prevailing idea across
the country, Germany would eventually decide

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:47.220
to spinoff these sectors into public companies
at the beginning of 1995, leading to the creation

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.500
of Deutsche Telekom. Around the same time,
an American wireless communications company

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.590
called Western Wireless was starting to gain
a lot of traction. Western Wireless was founded

00:04:56.590 --> 00:05:02.840
in 1988 and they were an early mover when
it came to wireless technology. I mean, “wireless”

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:07.720
is literally in their name. Given that Western
Wireless didn’t have the infrastructure

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:12.210
to compete against AT&amp;T in the traditional
telephone market, they spent all of their

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:18.160
effort on the wireless market and more specifically
the rural wireless market which was regularly

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.800
overlooked by larger providers. It turns out
that this was actually super lucrative, and

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:29.270
Western Wireless would eventually grow to
compete in even non rural areas. In the meantime,

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.020
given that Deutsche Telekom was now a corporate
entity focused on profits, they would shift

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:38.750
their focus on aggressive expansion. They
expanded to basically every European country

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:44.720
you can think of from Austria and Croatia
to Slovakia and the UK, but for the purposes

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:50.389
of this video, we’ll focus on their expansion
to the US. This came in 2000 with the purchase

00:05:50.389 --> 00:05:56.020
of a subsidiary of Western Wireless called
Voicestream Wireless for a ridiculous $50.7

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:02.620
billion. Fortunately, they would negotiate
this down to $30 billion, but Voicestream

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:08.310
wasn’t worth anywhere near $30 billion either.
Deutsche Telekom bought the absolute top of

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:14.060
the dotcom bubble, and when the crash came
around, their stock would fall 90% and never

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:19.710
recover. But, on the brightside, they now
owned what we all know as T Mobile.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:25.560
GETTING DESTROYED BY AT&amp;T:
While Deutsche Telekom likely overpaid for

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:32.120
Voicestream by a full 10X, it wasn’t all
bad news. At the end of the day, Voicestream

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:37.030
was still growing rapidly and Deutsche Telekom
had a bunch of money that they could throw

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:42.669
at Voicestream to try to win the US market.
In the early 2000s this worked out great,

00:06:42.669 --> 00:06:48.680
and this is why we all remember the T mobile
jingle to this day. But, T mobile had one

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.530
major weakness which is that they weren’t
actually doing anything particularly noteworthy

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:59.360
or innovative. They were basically just riding
the wave of wireless adoption. And while this

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.039
worked out decently while the market was still
exponentially growing, the same could not

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:09.091
be said about the mature market. You see,
once the cool factor of having a cell phone

00:07:09.091 --> 00:07:14.789
wore off, people actually started to value
the quality of the connection. And let’s

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:20.470
just say, the quality of T Mobile was absolute
garbage. While this made customers quite mad

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:25.730
and irritated, it wasn’t enough to kill
T Mobile altogether. Something that was enough

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:32.169
to kill T Mobile though was AT&amp;T. AT&amp;T had
been watching this whole saga from the sidelines.

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:38.310
For the entirety of the 1900s, they were the
undisputed king of telecom. Even government

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:43.759
mandated breakups couldn’t destroy their
power. But, now, all of a sudden, some rural

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:49.020
wireless network owned by some Deutsche company
overseas was threatening AT&amp;T’s dominance

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:55.400
in their home country. This was unacceptable,
and AT&amp;T was determined to put T Mobile back

00:07:55.400 --> 00:08:01.650
in their place. But, to do this, AT&amp;T had
to make big bets, simply offering better service

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:06.570
wasn’t good enough. So, they looked back
at their history at looked at how they got

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:11.780
so big in the first place, and the answer
was that they were an unbreakable monopoly.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:17.540
They had to recreate this monopoly and the
perfect opportunity to do this came with the

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:24.280
iPhone. Apple wasn’t exactly an easy company
to work with, but AT&amp;T was willing to do whatever

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:29.520
it took. This included giving Apple full control
of the software, paying out Apple for every

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.659
iPhone that was sold, and probably dozens
of other clauses. But, in return Apple granted

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:40.690
AT&amp;T with exclusive rights to the iPhone meaning
that if you wanted to buy an iPhone in the

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:46.720
US, you had to buy it with an AT&amp;T connection.
This didn’t seem like a big deal given that

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:53.370
no one really knew how big the iPhone would
get, but looking back, this was likely AT&amp;T’s

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:59.250
best choice of the century as they were able
to rebuild their monopoly for nearly 4 years.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.950
It wasn’t till 2011 that Verizon would get
their hands on the iPhone. And Sprint would

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:08.540
also partner with Apple later that year. Back
in Germany, Deutsche Telekom had struck a

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:15.779
similar exclusivity deal with Apple like AT&amp;T,
but as for T Mobile US, well they were screwed.

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:21.570
They would essentially seek out a bailout
from AT&amp;T, but as we touched on at the beginning,

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:25.670
the government would block that from happening.
It seemed like T Mobile was gonna to share

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:30.970
the same fate as most of its dotcom peers.
In a last ditch effort, the company would

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:37.990
hire a new CEO named John Legere, and man,
this guy would do the impossible.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:44.140
THE RESURRECTION OF T-MOBILE:
John wasn’t you’re typical CEO. In fact,

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:49.310
he was the exact opposite and he wasn’t
afraid to admit that either. And the first

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:55.510
thing he noticed after he took over was the
overly diplomatic nature of the telecom industry.

00:09:55.510 --> 00:10:01.190
Everything was uptight, pristine, and closely
monitored. This was the way that AT&amp;T and

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:06.950
Verizon liked it, and so everyone followed
suit. But, this strategy clearly wasn’t

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:12.670
working for T Mobile. Not to mention, T Mobile
had none of the pristine advantages that AT&amp;T

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:17.690
or Verizon had. They didn’t have a deal
with Apple. They didn’t have a massive marketing

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:23.750
budget. And they didn’t have a strong network.
In fact, they had a notably crappy network.

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:30.390
But despite this, T Mobile was trying to act
like an equal peer to AT&amp;T and Verizon. So,

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:35.750
John decided to take the opposite approach.
Instead of trying to hide who they were, John

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.201
embraced it. He realized that people were
fed up with AT&amp;T’s predatory cell phone

00:10:40.201 --> 00:10:45.551
contracts with loads of fine print and dozens
of hidden fees. So, he made T Mobile into

00:10:45.551 --> 00:10:51.339
the “Un Carrier”. And the first step in
doing this was calling up Apple. John didn’t

00:10:51.339 --> 00:10:57.310
ask Apple to give them a juicy deal. In fact,
he asked for the opposite. He asked if they

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:03.390
could sell the iPhone for MSRP. Given that
this didn’t seem threatening to AT&amp;T or

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:09.680
Verizon, Apple had an easy time agreeing.
And with that, T Mobile finally got the iPhone

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:16.031
in 2013 and they embraced who they were. Yeah,
they were crappy, they were the cheap, and

00:11:16.031 --> 00:11:21.829
yes having a T Mobile connection would probably
hurt your social status. But, guess what,

00:11:21.829 --> 00:11:27.490
they’re not gonna overcharge you or screw
you over with the fine print. There was also

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:32.320
absolutely no contracts or cancellation fees.
And for tens of millions of Americans who

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:38.370
had been screwed over by AT&amp;T and Verizon
for decades, this is exactly what they wanted.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:44.339
But, this didn’t mean that people switched
over instantly. In fact, it was a rather slow

00:11:44.339 --> 00:11:50.000
burn because people didn’t quite trust T
Mobile. I mean, there’s no way that a carrier

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:56.149
was actually acting decent and reasonable
right? That breaks the laws of physics. So,

00:11:56.149 --> 00:12:01.769
where’s the catch. Well, there actually
was no catch, but the general public only

00:12:01.769 --> 00:12:05.930
started to believe this once their daring
friends and family confirmed that there was

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:12.100
no catch. This strategy destroyed T Mobiles
margins as they lost out on all of the predatory

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:18.019
revenue. AT&amp;T, for example, generally has
net margins of 10%. Meanwhile, T Mobile was

00:12:18.019 --> 00:12:24.170
barely able to crack net margins of 2-4%.
But, something that T Mobile had that no one

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:31.019
else did was happy customers and this translated
to exponential growth. In the last 10 years,

00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:36.690
T Mobile’s annual revenue has rocketed from
$5 billion per year to $80 billion per year.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:42.770
And today, T Mobile has a total of a 110 million
wireless subscribers which is about a ⅓

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:47.610
of the US population making them the world’s
largest telecom company.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:53.920
THE COMEBACK OF T-MOBILE:
While T Mobile has maintained their generous

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:58.260
customer policies, they haven’t been as
stubborn when it comes to maintaining their

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:02.900
crappy quality. Instead, they’ve invested
much of their extra revenue back into growing

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:07.680
their infrastructure and making the service
better. And today, they’re actually the

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:13.329
leader when it comes to 5G service. According
to some research done by Opensignal, the average

00:13:13.329 --> 00:13:19.181
5G download speed for T-Mobile was 150 Mbps.
This was three times higher than AT&amp;T and

00:13:19.181 --> 00:13:26.550
Verizon who scored about 50 Mbps. And speaking
from personal experience, 150 Mbps is actually

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:31.399
the lowside when it comes to T Mobile’s
5G speeds. If you live in popular suburbs

00:13:31.399 --> 00:13:37.460
or urban areas, you can expect much faster
speeds. For example, I live in Austin, TX

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:43.010
and I regularly get download speeds above
600 Mbps. I mean, that’s literally fiber

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:48.720
speeds right there. So, clearly, T Mobile
is no longer just killing it in the affordability

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:54.329
sector, they’re also killing it in the performacne
sector as well. And that’s how T Mobie went

00:13:54.329 --> 00:13:59.160
from being a legacy German company who tried
to win using their power and influence to

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:05.029
being the underdog favorite to being the global
champion. It looks like doing the right thing

00:14:05.029 --> 00:14:09.570
does end up paying off from time to time.
Do you like your carrier? Comment that down

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:16.029
below. Also drop a like if all carriers were
as decent as T Mobile. And of course, consider

00:14:16.029 --> 00:14:19.770
checking out our discord community to suggest
